      "content": "**Section 1.5 — Deduction**  \nSection 1.5 of *Foundations of Computation* explains how logic lets us derive conclusions from premises. A premise is any proposition that is known to be true or that we accept as true for the sake of argument; a conclusion is a proposition that can be logically inferred from the premises. If the premises are true, logic forces the conclusion to be true as well.\n\nThe section introduces the standard *argument* format\n\n```\nIf today is Tuesday, then this is Belgium",
